1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,650
こんなに長い日本語の文章をどうやって読むの？

2
00:00:04,640 --> 00:00:06,580
How do Japanese people read such

3
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:09,350
a long sentence, without spaces?

4
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:11,500
In English, spaces tell us where

5
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:14,060
one word ends, and the next begins.

6
00:00:14,080 --> 00:00:15,270
But Japanese?

7
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:17,220
Words often run together,

8
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:18,390
without spaces.

9
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:20,380
So how do readers know where one

10
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:22,550
word ends, and another begins?

11
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:24,780
The first clue is kanji.

12
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:26,150
Look at this sentence.

13
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:28,790
Watashi wa nihongo o benkyō shimasu.

14
00:00:29,190 --> 00:00:31,410
Kanji carries important meaning.

15
00:00:31,430 --> 00:00:33,370
When readers see "nihongo",

16
00:00:33,390 --> 00:00:34,290
they can recognize the

17
00:00:34,310 --> 00:00:36,290
word for Japanese language.

18
00:00:36,310 --> 00:00:38,130
Next comes hiragana.

19
00:00:38,150 --> 00:00:40,770
Characters like "wa", "o", and

20
00:00:40,790 --> 00:00:42,930
"shimasu" help readers understand

21
00:00:42,950 --> 00:00:44,690
how the sentence is structured.

22
00:00:44,710 --> 00:00:46,650
They show grammatical relationships,

23
00:00:46,670 --> 00:00:48,530
and how words work together.

24
00:00:48,550 --> 00:00:49,740
And katakana?

25
00:00:49,830 --> 00:00:51,490
It often helps readers recognize

26
00:00:51,510 --> 00:00:53,690
foreign-origin words, names,

27
00:00:53,710 --> 00:00:55,890
and certain emphasized expressions.

28
00:00:55,910 --> 00:00:58,700
For example, "kōhī" means coffee.

29
00:00:58,870 --> 00:01:00,770
But here's the real secret.

30
00:01:00,790 --> 00:01:01,970
Japanese readers don't

31
00:01:01,990 --> 00:01:03,730
rely on just one clue.

32
00:01:03,750 --> 00:01:06,210
They combine kanji, kana,

33
00:01:06,230 --> 00:01:07,770
grammar, vocabulary,

34
00:01:07,790 --> 00:01:09,490
and context, to recognize

35
00:01:09,510 --> 00:01:11,490
meaningful chunks of a sentence.

36
00:01:11,510 --> 00:01:12,610
Their brains learn to see

37
00:01:12,630 --> 00:01:14,250
the boundaries, even when

38
00:01:14,270 --> 00:01:15,730
spaces aren't written.

39
00:01:15,750 --> 00:01:17,250
At first, Japanese can

40
00:01:17,270 --> 00:01:19,330
look like one endless line.

41
00:01:19,350 --> 00:01:20,210
But once you understand

42
00:01:20,230 --> 00:01:21,730
the patterns, the sentence

43
00:01:21,750 --> 00:01:23,370
starts to make sense.

44
00:01:23,390 --> 00:01:25,090
Follow me to learn Japanese,

45
00:01:25,110 --> 00:01:26,780
one step at a time.

46
00:01:27,430 --> 00:01:31,630
最後まで見てくれてありがとう。またね！
(Thanks for watching till the end. See you next time!)
